### What is linked list traversal?

Traversal is the most fundamental linked list operation. It means visiting every node exactly once by starting at the head and following each node's `next` reference until you reach `null`. You declare a local pointer called `current`, set it to `head`, and loop while `current` is not null. Inside the loop you process the current node's data, and at the end of each iteration you advance with `current = current.next`.

```java
package com.bank.core;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class TransactionNode {
    String txnId;
    BigDecimal amount;
    TransactionNode next;

    public TransactionNode(String txnId, BigDecimal amount) {
        this.txnId = txnId;
        this.amount = amount;
        this.next = null;
    }
}
```

### How do you traverse a linked list to print all nodes?

The basic pattern sets `current` to `head` and loops while `current` is not `null`. Each iteration processes the node and then advances the pointer.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import com.bank.core.TransactionNode;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class TransactionListPrinter {
    public static void printAll(TransactionNode head) {
        TransactionNode current = head;
        while (current != null) {
            System.out.println(current.txnId + " : " + current.amount);
            current = current.next;
        }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        TransactionNode head = new TransactionNode("TXN-001", new BigDecimal("5000"));
        head.next = new TransactionNode("TXN-002", new BigDecimal("12000"));
        head.next.next = new TransactionNode("TXN-003", new BigDecimal("8500"));

        printAll(head);
    }
}
```

```text
TXN-001 : 5000
TXN-002 : 12000
TXN-003 : 8500
```

### Why must you never move the head pointer during traversal?

A critical rule is never to move `head` itself during traversal. The `head` must keep pointing at the first node so the list can be used again later. If you reassign `head` to `head.next` repeatedly, you permanently lose the start of the list.

```java
// WRONG: loses the list permanently
public static void printWrong(TransactionNode head) {
    while (head != null) {
        System.out.println(head.txnId);
        head = head.next; // head is now lost after the loop!
    }
    // head is null here, list is unreachable
}
```

```java
// CORRECT: head stays intact
public static void printCorrect(TransactionNode head) {
    TransactionNode current = head;
    while (current != null) {
        System.out.println(current.txnId);
        current = current.next;
    }
    // head still points to the first node
}
```

### How do you use traversal to compute a sum?

Traversal underlies many operations: searching, counting, summing, and collecting. To total up all pending transaction amounts, you walk the chain and accumulate into a `BigDecimal` sum.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import com.bank.core.TransactionNode;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class TransactionSummer {
    public static BigDecimal totalPending(TransactionNode head) {
        BigDecimal sum = BigDecimal.ZERO;
        TransactionNode current = head;
        while (current != null) {
            sum = sum.add(current.amount);
            current = current.next;
        }
        return sum;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        TransactionNode head = new TransactionNode("TXN-001", new BigDecimal("5000.50"));
        head.next = new TransactionNode("TXN-002", new BigDecimal("12000.75"));
        head.next.next = new TransactionNode("TXN-003", new BigDecimal("8500.00"));

        System.out.println("Total pending: " + totalPending(head));
    }
}
```

```text
Total pending: 25501.25
```

### How do you search for a specific node during traversal?

To find a node matching a condition, you traverse and check each node. Return the node when found, or `null` if you reach the end without a match.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import com.bank.core.TransactionNode;

public class TransactionFinder {
    public static TransactionNode findById(TransactionNode head, String targetId) {
        TransactionNode current = head;
        while (current != null) {
            if (current.txnId.equals(targetId)) {
                return current;
            }
            current = current.next;
        }
        return null; // not found
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        TransactionNode head = new TransactionNode("TXN-001", new java.math.BigDecimal("5000"));
        head.next = new TransactionNode("TXN-002", new java.math.BigDecimal("12000"));

        TransactionNode found = findById(head, "TXN-002");
        if (found != null) {
            System.out.println("Found: " + found.txnId + " amount " + found.amount);
        } else {
            System.out.println("Not found");
        }
    }
}
```

```text
Found: TXN-002 amount 12000
```

### How do you traverse a doubly linked list backward?

A doubly linked list allows backward traversal using the `prev` reference. Start from the tail and follow `prev` pointers until you reach `null`.

```java
package com.bank.core;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class DoublyTransactionNode {
    String txnId;
    BigDecimal amount;
    DoublyTransactionNode prev;
    DoublyTransactionNode next;

    public DoublyTransactionNode(String txnId, BigDecimal amount) {
        this.txnId = txnId;
        this.amount = amount;
    }
}
```

```java
package com.bank.service;

import com.bank.core.DoublyTransactionNode;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class ReverseTraversal {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        DoublyTransactionNode a = new DoublyTransactionNode("TXN-001", new BigDecimal("5000"));
        DoublyTransactionNode b = new DoublyTransactionNode("TXN-002", new BigDecimal("12000"));
        DoublyTransactionNode c = new DoublyTransactionNode("TXN-003", new BigDecimal("8500"));
        a.next = b; b.prev = a;
        b.next = c; c.prev = b;

        // Backward traversal from tail
        System.out.println("Reverse order:");
        DoublyTransactionNode current = c; // tail
        while (current != null) {
            System.out.println("  " + current.txnId + " : " + current.amount);
            current = current.prev;
        }
    }
}
```

```text
Reverse order:
  TXN-003 : 8500
  TXN-002 : 12000
  TXN-001 : 5000
```

### What are the edge cases in traversal?

The most important edge case is an empty list where `head` is `null`. The `while (current != null)` guard handles this naturally because the loop body never executes. A single-node list also works because the loop runs once and then `current.next` is `null`, ending the loop.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import com.bank.core.TransactionNode;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class TraversalEdgeCases {
    public static int countNodes(TransactionNode head) {
        int count = 0;
        TransactionNode current = head;
        while (current != null) {
            count++;
            current = current.next;
        }
        return count;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Empty list count: " + countNodes(null));

        TransactionNode single = new TransactionNode("TXN-001", new BigDecimal("5000"));
        System.out.println("Single node count: " + countNodes(single));

        single.next = new TransactionNode("TXN-002", new BigDecimal("12000"));
        System.out.println("Two node count: " + countNodes(single));
    }
}
```

```text
Empty list count: 0
Single node count: 1
Two node count: 2
```

### What is the time and space complexity of traversal?

Traversal visits each of the n nodes exactly once, so the time complexity is O(n). It uses only a single `current` pointer variable regardless of how many nodes the list has, so the space complexity is O(1). This makes traversal the most memory-efficient way to process a linked list.

| Operation | Time | Space |
|---|---|---|
| Print all nodes | O(n) | O(1) |
| Sum all values | O(n) | O(1) |
| Search for a value | O(n) worst case | O(1) |
| Count nodes | O(n) | O(1) |

```java
// Every traversal pattern follows this template
TransactionNode current = head;
while (current != null) {
    // process current node
    current = current.next;
}
```

### What should a senior engineer know about traversal?

A senior engineer knows that traversal is the building block of nearly every linked list algorithm. Cycle detection, reversal, merging, and finding the middle node all rely on traversal. The two-pointer technique (slow and fast pointers) is a traversal variant where two pointers advance at different speeds.

In production, the key concern is guarding against cycles. If a corrupted linked list has a cycle, a naive traversal runs forever. Floyd's tortoise-and-hare algorithm detects cycles during traversal using O(1) extra space.

```java
// Guard against cycles during traversal using a step limit
public static void safePrint(TransactionNode head, int maxSteps) {
    TransactionNode current = head;
    int steps = 0;
    while (current != null && steps < maxSteps) {
        System.out.println(current.txnId);
        current = current.next;
        steps++;
    }
    if (current != null) {
        System.out.println("WARNING: traversal stopped at step limit, possible cycle");
    }
}
```
